## DC 5 — Controls (CC1 – CC9)

### Edit 5.CC6.1 💡 — Strengthen the access-control description

The current CC6 bullets are generic. The actual access model in code is more rigorous than the description; making it explicit pre-empts auditor probes.

**Add (or expand the existing JML/MFA/encryption bullets)**:

> - End-user authentication into the platform is delegated to identity providers: Microsoft Entra ID (per-tenant) for all customers, Google Workspace OAuth for the platform tenant, and email-and-password for the platform tenant only. Multi-factor authentication is enforced at the identity-provider level. Sessions expire after seven days with one-day refresh intervals.
> - Engineer access to the Azure environment is delegated to Microsoft Entra ID with Privileged Identity Management (PIM). The AKS cluster has local admin accounts disabled and uses Entra-backed Kubernetes RBAC. PostgreSQL access requires Entra AD authentication; developer access to a customer-tenant database requires PIM activation of a per-tenant Entra group, and all developer queries are captured by `pgaudit` with the developer's user principal name recorded in the log line prefix.
> - Workloads authenticate to Azure resources using federated workload identities (per-tenant Kubernetes service accounts federated to per-purpose Azure user-assigned managed identities) — no long-lived secrets are stored in pods for Azure-resource access. Application secrets are mounted from Azure Key Vault via the Secrets Store CSI Driver.

**Truth source**: §§5, 6, 7 of `system-map-from-code.md`.

